\title{Proofs}
\date{2026-02-09T11:35:39Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{We can formally establish the truth of a proposition by way of a \em{proof}.  Proofs can be written in a more or less formal \em{style}, but the underlying structure is always the same.  We will consider each connective of propositional logic, and see for each (1) how to \em{prove} it, and (2) how to \em{use} it if you know it.  (How to prove and use a given connective are also known as \em{introduction} and \em{elimination} rules, respectively.)}

\p{You need to memorize these; but they should make some intuitive sense, so memorizing them is not difficult.}

\p{Once you understand how to prove and use each connective, writing a proof for a mathematical statement looks something like this:
  \ol{
    \li{Translate the statement into propositional logic (using #{\land}, #{\lor}, #{\neg}, #{\implies}, #{\forall}, and #{\exists}).}
    \li{Write a proof outline corresponding to the propositional logic formula, using the rules for proof and use of the relevant connectives.}
    \li{Use your intuition/insight/ingenuity/specific content knowledge
  to fill in the missing bits.}
  }
This might feel a bit facetious (cf [How To Draw An Owl meme](https://i.kym-cdn.com/photos/images/original/000/572/078/d6d.jpg)), but also
serious: step 2 is mechanical, and gets us a lot of the way there;
step 3 is the step that requires some creativity/insight.
}

